      "quote": "A road that should help can make everyone worse off.",
      "attribution": "Braess's paradox in one sentence"
    },
    {
      "id": "t3-step-003",
      "type": "stepReveal",
      "durationInFrames": 623,
      "narration": "The paradox happens because each driver acts rationally in their own interest. The new connector changes the incentives — the hybrid route always looks faster to any individual. But when everyone makes the same rational choice, both variable legs congest simultaneously. What is optimal for one becomes worse for all.",
      "transition": {
        "type": "slide",
        "durationInFrames": 20,
        "direction": "from-left"
      },
      "title": "Why the paradox happens",
      "body": [
        "Each driver acts rationally in their own interest",
        "The hybrid route always looks faster to any individual",
        "When everyone chooses it, both variable legs congest simultaneously",
        "What is optimal for one becomes worse for all"
      ],
